<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  CHUNKED upload into a password-protected Nextcloud public share, for a big collection.

  TWO THINGS TO REPLACE:

    SHARETOKEN        what follows /s/ in the share URL. Appears FOUR times, and must be written
                      literally: a <rest> step's 'path' is NOT environment-expanded.
    SHARE-PASSWORD    the share password.

  THE AUTHENTICATION IS NOT THE LOGIN DIALOG of rest-nextcloud-form.xml, and it cannot be.
  On a public share the share TOKEN is the Basic username and the share PASSWORD is the Basic
  password, so one preemptive Basic credential covers every request. That matters because
  /public.php/dav/uploads/ - the collection chunked upload needs - answers 401 to a session cookie
  no matter which headers accompany it, and 201 to the same request under Basic. Measured on the
  wire, not assumed.

  SO IF YOUR DEPLOYMENT DROPS THE Authorization HEADER, THIS CONFIG CANNOT WORK, and neither can any
  other chunked upload into that share. The symptom is a 401 whose body carries an empty
  <s:message/> and whose response has no WWW-Authenticate header: that is Nextcloud saying no
  credential arrived, not that the credential was wrong. Check CGIPassAuth On (Apache) or
  fastcgi_pass_header Authorization (nginx with PHP-FPM) with whoever owns the front end. Fixing it
  also removes the need for the login dialog altogether, so it is worth asking.

  Nextcloud chunked upload v2, three steps:

    1. MKCOL an upload collection named by a random id. {upload.random:hex:16} resolves to the same
       value in all three steps of one file, and to a fresh one per file.
    2. PUT each chunk under a name that IS its byte offset. Nextcloud sorts them numerically, not
       lexicographically, so 1024 follows 512 rather than sorting between 1 and 2.
    3. MOVE the collection's virtual '.file' onto the destination. That is what assembles it.

  chunk_size is 10 MiB here. Nextcloud's own clients use 10 MiB; the practical ceiling is whatever
  the front end allows for one request body (php.ini upload_max_filesize / post_max_size, and any
  proxy limit), which is precisely the limit chunking exists to stay under.

  X-Requested-With is sent on every step because public DAV refuses any non-GET without it unless the
  instance enables outgoing server-to-server sharing. Harmless where it is not required.

  DELIBERATELY NO Content-Range HEADER, unlike every other chunked fixture here. In the generic-HTTP
  ones (rest-http-chunked.xml and the proxy fixtures) the chunks all share ONE path and Content-Range is
  the only thing telling the sink where each one goes. Here the URL carries the offset instead, each
  chunk is a whole resource of its own, and Sabre refuses any PUT that arrives with a Content-Range:

      PUT /public.php/dav/uploads/<token>/<id>/300   (no Content-Range)      -> 201
      PUT /public.php/dav/uploads/<token>/<id>/300   + Content-Range         -> 400 BadRequest

  So adding the header here does not harden anything, it breaks every chunk. Measured, not assumed.

  Certificate pinning is not set. Add server_cert_pin="<base64 of the certificate>" to <upload>; do
  not use insecure="true", the Basic credential is the share password.
-->
<dfir-orc>
    <upload method="REST" server="https://SHARE-HOST"
            user="SHARETOKEN" password="SHARE-PASSWORD" authscheme="Basic"
            https_security="tls1.3, tls1.2" operation="copy">

        <rest http_method="MKCOL" path="/public.php/dav/uploads/SHARETOKEN/{upload.random:hex:16}">
            <header>X-Requested-With: XMLHttpRequest</header>
        </rest>

        <rest http_method="PUT"
              path="/public.php/dav/uploads/SHARETOKEN/{upload.random:hex:16}/{upload.range_start}"
              body="{upload.local_path}" chunk_size="10485760">
            <header>X-Requested-With: XMLHttpRequest</header>
        </rest>

        <rest http_method="MOVE" path="/public.php/dav/uploads/SHARETOKEN/{upload.random:hex:16}/.file">
            <header>Destination: /public.php/dav/files/SHARETOKEN/{upload.filename}</header>
            <header>X-Requested-With: XMLHttpRequest</header>
        </rest>

    </upload>
</dfir-orc>
